## Wrapping native elements — do not override `CustomEventHandlersCamelCase`

When a component renders a native element (`<dialog>`, `<button>`, `<input>`, …), extend the matching `JSX.*HtmlAttributes<T>`. That type already includes `CustomEventHandlersCamelCase` (`onClick`, `onClose`, `onInput`, …).

**Do not redeclare `on*` handlers on the wrapper Props.** Pass them through `{...others}` to the native element.

Bad — overrides the DOM handler type:

```tsx
type Props = JSX.DialogHtmlAttributes<HTMLDialogElement> & {
  onClose?: () => void;
};
```

Good — only add props the wrapper itself interprets; spread the rest:

```tsx
type Props = JSX.DialogHtmlAttributes<HTMLDialogElement> & {
  id: string;
  action?: (formData: FormData) => Promise<boolean>;
};

const [local, others] = splitProps(props, ["children", "action"]);
return <dialog {...others} on:close={resetForm} />;
```

Callers pass DOM handlers directly; `() => void` is assignable to `EventHandlerUnion`:

```tsx
<Modal id="menu-modal" onClose={() => setOpen(false)} />
```

Notes:
- Do not `splitProps(..., ["onClose", ...])` for handlers that spread already forwards — unless explicitly requested.
- Do not use `addEventListener` on `getElementById` when the same handler can be passed as a JSX prop.
- An internal function named `onClose` (or similar) for wrapper-only logic (e.g. form reset) is not a reason to add a public Prop with the same name.
